# Project Structure Awareness

## Purpose

Require the agent to inspect and follow the existing project structure before creating files, folders, services, components, or configuration.

## When This Applies

- Any new feature
- Any refactor
- New files or folders
- Moving code
- Adding configuration
- Changing application architecture

## Required Workflow

1. Inspect the repository tree before editing.
2. Find existing patterns for similar features.
3. Place new files beside the closest existing equivalent.
4. Reuse established naming conventions.
5. Update imports through the existing barrel or module pattern when present.

## Standards

- Follow the current folder architecture unless there is a clear reason to improve it.
- Prefer small targeted edits over broad rewrites.
- Respect app, src, components, server, services, database, and tests conventions.
- Keep generated code discoverable.
- Use consistent naming across files, exports, routes, and tests.

## Do Not

- Do not create a parallel architecture.
- Do not add top level folders when an existing folder fits.
- Do not scatter related files across unrelated directories.
- Do not rename public APIs without updating all references.
- Do not assume Next.js, Vite, FastAPI, or another framework without checking files first.

## Verification Checklist

- The existing project pattern was inspected before changes were made.
- The implementation is scoped to the user request.
- Unsafe files, secrets, and unrelated changes are excluded.
- Applicable lint, typecheck, test, build, or validation commands were run when available.
- Any skipped or failed verification is reported clearly.

## Related Skills

- Use the matching `.cursor/skills/` workflow when one exists for this rule.
- When no matching skill exists, follow this rule directly and keep changes minimal, safe, and verifiable.
